---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const speeds = [
  ['SD (standard definition)', '5 Mbps', '10 Mbps', '15 Mbps'],
  ['HD', '10 Mbps', '20 Mbps', '30 Mbps'],
  ['FHD (Full HD 1080p)', '15 Mbps', '30 Mbps', '45 Mbps'],
  ['4K (Ultra HD)', '25 Mbps', '50 Mbps', '75 Mbps'],
];
---
<Layout title="Prime IPTV Availability & Internet Speed Requirements" description="Prime IPTV serves viewers across Canada and is also available worldwide. See the speed needed for SD, HD, FHD and 4K streaming on one or more screens.">
  <Header slot="header" />
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:820px;">
      <div style="text-align:center;">
        <div class="section-label"><Icon name="globe" size={14} />Availability</div>
        <h1 class="section-title">Prime IPTV for Viewers in Canada and Worldwide</h1>
        <p style="color:var(--muted); margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Prime IPTV serves customers in <strong style="color:var(--text);">Canada</strong> first and is available across all provinces and territories. It is also <strong style="color:var(--text);">available worldwide</strong>, so viewers outside Canada can use the service too.</p>
        <div class="coverage-badge"><Icon name="flag" size={16} /> All provinces and territories · Also available worldwide</div>
      </div>

      <h2><Icon name="wifi" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> What internet speed do you need for IPTV and 4K streaming?</h2>
      <p>The internet speed for streaming IPTV depends on the picture quality and the number of screens. Prime IPTV streams over your own internet connection, so speed and stability matter more than where you live. The table shows a rough guide to the speed each stream needs, and what that adds up to when two or three screens watch at once. These are rules of thumb, not guarantees. Real needs vary with your device, player and what else is using your connection.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="speed-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>1 screen</th><th>2 screens</th><th>3 screens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {speeds.map(([q, a, b, c]) => <tr><td><strong>{q}</strong></td><td>{a}</td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td></tr>)}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2><Icon name="sliders" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> Get the most out of your connection</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Test at the time you watch.</strong> Run a speed test in the evening on the device you use, not just on a computer next to the router.</li>
        <li><strong>Prefer a cable.</strong> Ethernet is steadier than Wi-Fi, especially for 4K and live sports.</li>
        <li><strong>Count your screens.</strong> Plans cover 1 to 4 connections. See <a href="/blog/how-many-iptv-connections-do-i-need/">how many connections you need</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick the right quality.</strong> If your connection is limited, choose HD or FHD channels instead of 4K.</li>
        <li><strong>Fix problems step by step.</strong> Our <a href="/blog/iptv-buffering-troubleshooting/">buffering and login checklist</a> covers the usual causes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2><Icon name="search" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> How to test your internet speed</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Connect the device you actually watch on, or a phone on the same Wi-Fi, to the network you will use.</li>
        <li>Run a speed test in a browser or app at the time you normally watch. Evenings are usually the busiest.</li>
        <li>Pause other heavy use first (downloads, cloud backups, other streams) for a clean result.</li>
        <li>Compare the download speed with the table above, multiplied by the number of screens you plan to run together.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2><Icon name="wifi" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> Wi-Fi or Ethernet?</h2>
      <p>Ethernet gives the steadiest connection and is the best choice for TVs and streaming boxes that have a port, particularly for 4K and live sport. Wi-Fi works well when the signal is strong, but distance, walls and a crowded network reduce real-world speed. If you use Wi-Fi, stay on the 5 GHz band where you can and keep the device as close to the router as practical. Fire TV devices can use an Ethernet adapter.</p>

      <h2><Icon name="sliders" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> How many screens can your connection handle?</h2>
      <p>Add up the speed each screen needs. For example, two 4K streams (about 25 Mbps each) plus one HD stream (about 10 Mbps) is roughly 60 Mbps, and you should leave headroom for other people using the network. If your connection cannot carry that, lower the quality on some screens or choose a plan with fewer simultaneous connections. The <a href="/blog/how-many-iptv-connections-do-i-need/">connection chooser</a> helps you decide.</p>

      <h2><Icon name="bulb" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> Quick router and network tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Restart the router if speeds drop, and place it in an open, central spot.</li>
        <li>Move the streaming device away from microwaves and thick walls.</li>
        <li>Disconnect devices you are not using from the Wi-Fi.</li>
        <li>If you use a VPN and notice buffering, switch it off briefly to test.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2><Icon name="globe" size={22} class="ic-accent" /> Watching from outside Canada</h2>
      <p>The same service works for viewers worldwide. Your experience still depends on your local internet connection, so use the speed guide above. For help choosing a plan or setting up in your country, message us on <a href="https://wa.me/14062356618" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WhatsApp</a> or <a href="/contact/">contact support</a>.</p>

      <div class="cta-row">
        <a href="/free-trial/" class="btn-primary"><Icon name="play" size={14} />Start 24-Hour Free Trial</a>
        <a href="/pricing/" class="btn-outline">View Pricing<Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a>
        <a href="/setup/" class="btn-outline">Setup Guides<Icon name="arrow" size={16} /></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>
<style>
  h1 { font-size:clamp(1.75rem,4vw,2.5rem); font-weight:900; margin:0 0 1rem; }
  h2 { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:0.5rem; font-size:1.3rem; font-weight:800; margin:2.5rem 0 0.75rem; }
  p { color:var(--muted); line-height:1.75; margin:0 0 1rem; }
  ul { color:var(--muted); padding-left:1.4rem; margin:0 0 1.25rem; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:0.5rem; line-height:1.7; }
  ul a, p a { color:var(--brand); font-weight:600; }
  strong { color:var(--text); }
  .coverage-badge { display:inline-flex; align-items:center; gap:0.5rem; background:rgba(11,42,111,0.1); border:1px solid rgba(11,42,111,0.3); color:var(--brand); padding:0.7rem 1.5rem; border-radius:10px; font-size:1rem; font-weight:700; }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x:auto; }
  .speed-table { width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; font-size:0.92rem; }
  .speed-table th, .speed-table td { border:1px solid var(--border); padding:0.65rem 0.9rem; text-align:left; color:var(--muted); }
  .speed-table th { background:var(--bg-2); color:var(--text); }
  .cta-row { display:flex; gap:1rem; flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top:2rem; }
</style>
